WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.380
You'll never have to stand in line to play Double Dragon again!

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:26.080
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:27.220
I'm Mike.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:28.240
And I'm Sean.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:30.360
And that's it. That's just us today.

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:31.540
When was the last time?

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:33.320
It's been a while. It has been a minute.

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:39.900
You know, like I always find these to be much more intimate and sensual, especially when like the studio lights dim a little bit.

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:46.600
A lot of people have written into the show and said that they prefer it when all four of us are on the show.

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:48.240
There are some things we can't control.

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:54.160
So if you don't feel that way and you're like, oh, I love it when it's just Mike and Sean, maybe write in to make us feel better about ourselves.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:56.140
We'll just cut the rest of them out of the show.

00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:57.340
Yeah, I don't see.

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:04.840
Yeah. In fact, yeah, we'll fill it in with just more of like a different persona of me and a different persona of you and that'll still get us to four.

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:08.660
Yeah, totally. And that's all that they really want is four voices.

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:14.020
Would you be like, yeah, that's all they were ever asking for was I like it better when more people talk all at the same time.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:15.600
Incoherently. We do that a lot.

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:18.420
So, Sean, today we're playing Double Dragon.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:21.600
True. That's made by Technos Japan.

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:27.040
And if you may remember, Technos Japan has released one other game on the NES for us so far.

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:28.140
Do you know what that game might be?

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:29.740
Renegade. Renegade, right.

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:32.980
The other beat-em-up game, the only other one really.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:34.140
The proto-beat-em-up.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:41.620
Proto-beat-em-up, yeah. This game is very much the spiritual and technological successor to Renegade.

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:43.440
And I think we're going to see that a lot.

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:49.580
But that makes me think like, well, we only played Renegade like that was only like a couple episodes ago, right?

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:52.720
I think it was more than a couple, but I know what you're saying.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:55.820
Yeah, I'm saying like maybe 20 episodes ago.

00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:04.440
Sure. You know, so it hasn't been much time, but you can see in Double Dragon that a lot of improvements have been made in this genre.

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:06.060
It's like they listened to the episode.

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:07.540
Right. Well, they did.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:11.740
They listened to the episode, went back in time and said, oh, we can make a better game.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:12.740
Let's call it Double Dragon. Yeah.

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:15.360
And then, you know, movies, cartoons later.

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:16.600
Everything ensues.

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:25.660
Yeah. Just for those of you that don't know, Double Dragon is a side-scrolling beat-em-up where you attack enemies on screen with punches and kicks.

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:28.280
And eventually you can learn other things, too.

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:31.080
In the arcade version, you just had them all already.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:44.620
But the important thing here is that makes this a little different than, say, Renegade is that it's a true, like, side-scroller from left to right where you have to clear the enemies on the screen and continue moving for, like, forward progression.

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:51.540
Whereas in Renegade, you were stuck to a screen and then you move to, like, another screen to face the next wave.

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:58.820
It's like the codifier of the actual genre that you're thinking of when you say beat-em-up.

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:03.440
Right. This is really close to, like, Streets of Rage level of the beat-em-up genre.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:08.460
Yeah. Which will eventually, like, give everybody their official definition of what a beat-em-up should be.

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:09.280
And all those bad dudes.

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:12.100
Yeah. You know, I'm a pretty bad dude.

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:14.320
I know. Are you a bad enough dude to rescue the president?

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:19.280
Always. We shouldn't have spoiled that before that episode, but we'll get back to that.

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:27.340
I don't think we really need to do any preliminary talking here because we set up a lot of what a beat-em-up can and should be in the Renegade episode.

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:29.140
So let's just get right into it.

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:31.020
Double Dragon, you boot up the game.

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:38.840
How are you feeling? You know, after I go through that, what seems like way too many options for the beginning,

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:51.540
I get into the game and I see some very effective motivational cutscene to go forward where somebody just, like, punches the girlfriend in the stomach and then you're off.

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:55.840
Yeah. And it's good graphics for the NES.

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:57.260
Yeah. This is very impressive.

00:03:57.580 --> 00:03:59.480
Yeah. So I'm already, like, engaged.

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:05.880
I think it's very clear that there's a very simple story to be told and now you just go right into the fun.

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:10.840
And the fun, of course, is punching and kicking everybody that stands in your way.

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:13.760
Yeah. Only people that stand in your way ever show up in this game.

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:16.860
The streets have been cleared for everybody but villains.

00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:20.480
How did you feel about just, like, your general attacking power?

00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:30.740
This felt, like, just from the weight of the punches, the rate of movement, like, this just felt like how it felt it should.

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:34.060
Yeah. Everything connects very easily.

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:40.000
I'd say there's a couple of, like, later moves that feel like, oh, that probably should have hit.

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:42.240
When it's most, like, directional inputs and stuff like that.

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:51.000
Right, right. But for the most part, like, a punch is satisfying because there's also animation for both parties, like, you when it happens to you when you get punched.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:57.420
And when you punch an enemy, there's a satisfying, like, connection that happens that registers a hit.

00:04:57.880 --> 00:04:59.820
It knocks them back or knocks you back.

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:07.700
And that split second delay between the punch and their, like, slow motion fallback, that just feels right.

00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:11.960
It has crunchiness and it has weight to it.

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:13.260
So it feels good. Yeah.

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:19.900
And the progression of the enemies, I mean, you meet a lot of what you're going to see through most of the game just in the first level.

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:26.280
Yes. And that actually didn't bug me because it's only four missions and it does get pretty tough.

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:36.020
So I didn't mind having not so much, like, new encounters to deal with that were going to be, like, incredibly hard things that would then set me back to stage one again.

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:45.460
Yeah. I think that there isn't really much tutorializing here, but they do give you, like, the easier enemies to begin with.

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:48.140
And that's, you know, just typical video game logic.

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:53.320
Yeah. The one thing they didn't that they do tutorialize, but they don't ever spell out for you.

00:05:53.420 --> 00:06:03.840
So if you don't own a manual or don't have a manual and that's jumping there, there comes a point, I think, in stage two where a broken bridge is in your is in your way.

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:07.340
And if you walk up to it, you'll fall into the river.

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:09.180
But if you don't get out.

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:11.580
No, you can't get out. You'll lose your life.

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:19.160
Okay. But they never told you, like, about jumping and you never had to jump before that you had to climb fences and stuff.

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:27.520
Yeah. So it was a weird moment where I never used the jump buttons before, but I said, okay, well, there has to be a way for me to jump.

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:31.660
It turns out you just push A and B together, but I had to refer to the manual for that.

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:33.280
So you didn't read the manual to start.

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:40.220
I did not read the manual to start because I assumed and was correct in thinking that one button would be to punch and one button would be to kick.

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:43.320
That was the problem in the prior game.

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:53.360
Yes. That in the prior game, for those of you that can remember, you had to, like, face it depending on which way you were facing that would do the different inputs.

00:06:53.660 --> 00:06:59.740
Yeah. So punching and kicking were tied to, like, in the right, if you're facing the right, A would punch.

00:06:59.740 --> 00:07:02.020
But if you were facing the left, A would kick.

00:07:02.020 --> 00:07:11.560
But basically, B would always attack to the right and A would always attack to the left and it just depended on whether it was going to be a punch or a kick.

00:07:11.740 --> 00:07:14.560
Right, right. So it was still confusing as hell.

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:15.880
They fixed that a lot.

00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:24.740
And I think they also kind of fixed that Z-axis that we were complaining about because maybe it's just because the graphics are better.

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:30.220
But I was able to better understand if I was just going to punch nothing for the character.

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:33.000
Yeah. And maybe it was just more forgiving, too, in terms of a hitbox.

00:07:33.140 --> 00:07:35.700
Like, you don't have to be exactly lined up with, like, this.

00:07:36.020 --> 00:07:37.860
Because if you think about it, if you're pixels, right?

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:39.280
If you're pixels, yeah.

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:41.640
Yeah, but I'm saying, like, you're just flat, you know?

00:07:41.660 --> 00:07:42.600
I know. It's really hard to see.

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:45.260
It's like these strips of paper, like, fighting each other.

00:07:45.260 --> 00:07:47.220
So you have to be, like, perfectly aligned for it to happen.

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:50.140
And this game's a little more forgiving than Renegade was.

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:55.720
Totally. Do you think that if you're pixels, like, do you just see, like, a line?

00:07:56.180 --> 00:07:58.040
Or do you see in two dimensions as well?

00:07:58.040 --> 00:08:03.560
Somebody made a 3D version or a first-person version of Super Mario Bros.

00:08:03.940 --> 00:08:05.580
Where you see through Mario's head.

00:08:05.780 --> 00:08:07.340
And it is, like, terrifying.

00:08:07.780 --> 00:08:10.460
And also what somebody would dream like, you know?

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:14.560
Nobody would actually ever have a good time experiencing that.

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:16.380
Yeah, in 2D land being pixels.

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:20.240
But, you know, at least now we have the technology.

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:21.320
We didn't have it back then.

00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:23.920
But now we have the technology to see what that would feel like.

00:08:24.180 --> 00:08:26.560
Science. I'll try to link that in the show notes for everybody.

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:28.660
But, yeah, that's actually a really cool thing.

00:08:28.700 --> 00:08:31.280
And there's another cool thing, if we're just going to get on tangents here for a minute.

00:08:31.780 --> 00:08:41.360
There's this, like, 3D NES emulator that, like, automatically calculates for certain games that they've programmed to make 3D.

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:42.580
So this way it plays better.

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:44.720
Sometimes it gets a little, like, mind-fucky.

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:50.620
Yeah. I think only, like, there's, like, maybe Metroid and Mega Man are, like, the two games that they've really spent time on.

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:54.840
Like, figuring out how to make all the pixelated elements work in 3D.

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:58.080
Yeah. But otherwise, if you just throw, like, a random game in there, I don't even know if it'll take it.

00:08:58.400 --> 00:08:59.260
It might try.

00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:05.040
Yeah. So one thing that we haven't talked about yet with attacking, though, is the leveling up system.

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:08.360
Yes. Now, this is non-existent in the arcade version.

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:11.880
In the arcade version, you already had everything in your repertoire.

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:17.220
And you just kind of, like, figured it out by either, like, looking around on the panel of the arcade.

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:18.440
It would show you all the moves.

00:09:18.700 --> 00:09:21.780
Or you would just do them, you know, naturally by button mashing.

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:26.260
Like, this game doesn't spell out for you the leveling up system.

00:09:26.300 --> 00:09:27.700
It's based on your hearts.

00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:29.620
Yes. Down at the bottom left.

00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:31.560
So there's no real sense of, like, levels.

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:33.400
It's just like, oh, now I have more hearts.

00:09:33.400 --> 00:09:34.300
I think, right?

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:37.500
Yeah. So basically, you have a score.

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:39.080
And that just counts up normally.

00:09:39.560 --> 00:09:41.640
And then that's in the bottom right.

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:46.920
And in the bottom left, you have this other number that goes up every time you land a hit.

00:09:47.980 --> 00:09:52.040
And once you hit a certain amount, once you get to a certain number, you get another heart.

00:09:52.220 --> 00:09:54.280
And that heart is basically leveling up.

00:09:54.580 --> 00:09:57.440
And with it comes a new move that you can do.

00:09:57.540 --> 00:10:00.240
It doesn't say, like, new move acquired or anything like that.

00:10:00.260 --> 00:10:04.000
You just know. Like, oh, this is the second heart move.

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:07.640
And the whole concept, I don't really know why it's in the game.

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:14.780
Because there are only so many enemies that you fight before getting at least to, like, level three.

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:22.760
And so I think that, like, everybody, unless you're more efficient, if you're more efficient, you're going to get less hearts.

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:26.760
Right. So I don't know what, like, the point of this was, but it's there.

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:33.700
Yeah, I think, just to go over it for a second, you have punch, kick, jump, and headbutt available to you right at the beginning.

00:10:33.780 --> 00:10:34.900
I love headbutt.

00:10:35.060 --> 00:10:36.100
Yeah, it looks pretty good at that.

00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:37.080
It's pretty cool.

00:10:37.480 --> 00:10:42.220
And then, like, level two comes around, and no additional inputs are required.

00:10:42.220 --> 00:10:46.620
But now you have the uppercut and a roundhouse kick on stunned opponents.

00:10:46.860 --> 00:10:57.940
Yes. I just feel like, you know, it seems like this was added to make the NES version a little harder because of the other limitations of the game that we'll get into.

00:10:58.140 --> 00:11:03.300
But there's no reason why, like, you know, you couldn't have those abilities at the beginning.

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:10.080
And it doesn't feel like, it doesn't, because there's no, like, awesome screen that tells you about your newly acquired techniques.

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:14.360
Or, like, you don't go to, like, a sensei who teaches you these things.

00:11:14.580 --> 00:11:19.420
Because it just happens randomly, all of a sudden, you'll just notice, like, oh, I have the uppercut.

00:11:19.580 --> 00:11:23.100
Yeah. Like, now your combo looks a little different.

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:27.360
And that's the only thing that really tips you off if you haven't read the manual.

00:11:28.780 --> 00:11:34.700
It does get a lot more, like, you get a lot more useful things down the road, but you have to figure out how to use them.

00:11:34.700 --> 00:11:41.400
Yeah. And I think, for what it's worth, though, these additional moves, I wouldn't mind if they were all available from the beginning.

00:11:41.440 --> 00:11:43.840
But they are great to have in this game.

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:53.080
It's a very welcome addition to this whole thing because we're so used to, even in, like, action games and stuff like that, only having, like, the A button does one thing.

00:11:53.240 --> 00:11:54.760
And maybe you get, like, different objects.

00:11:54.760 --> 00:11:56.440
Yeah. So it's treated slightly different.

00:11:56.480 --> 00:11:58.560
And then the B button is another thing.

00:11:58.660 --> 00:12:00.360
And then your D-pad up is to jump.

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:09.260
So to have, like, a lot of different mechanics here, to be able to, like, have a thing to grab somebody and then another button to throw them.

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:12.240
And then when they're on the ground, you can face punch them.

00:12:12.420 --> 00:12:14.660
Like, to have all that stuff is really cool.

00:12:14.860 --> 00:12:19.700
It makes it feel more like a complete game instead of whatever Renegade sort of was.

00:12:19.980 --> 00:12:26.740
Right. But you're kind of, like, leveling up so fast because there's only four missions that it just feels like, how are you learning this stuff?

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:28.860
Like, shouldn't you have just had all this to begin with?

00:12:28.860 --> 00:12:34.720
Like, if there's only four missions, is it really, like, is it really bad if I learn the elbow move from the beginning?

00:12:35.140 --> 00:12:39.080
No, I mean, yeah, it takes, what, like, ten minutes to get to, like, five?

00:12:39.440 --> 00:12:40.300
Like, I don't... Yeah, yeah.

00:12:40.380 --> 00:12:42.540
I think level five is very easy to get to.

00:12:42.600 --> 00:12:45.140
And then, like, level six requires some...

00:12:45.140 --> 00:12:50.100
But that's only because by level six, you're probably on mission 3.5.

00:12:50.240 --> 00:12:51.860
Right. And it's a pain in the ass now.

00:12:51.860 --> 00:12:54.980
So, I think that it...

00:12:54.980 --> 00:13:02.140
This is one of those artificial barriers that I wish they wouldn't have even thrown in there.

00:13:02.960 --> 00:13:06.500
I can see why they did, but I don't think it was a just reason.

00:13:06.880 --> 00:13:14.640
Yeah, it also feels like the timer was another thing that was not necessary in a home version of this game.

00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:21.980
There's no reason to tell me that, well, I spent too much time fighting these guys, so now the game's over.

00:13:22.220 --> 00:13:28.680
Like, why? Was she, like, tied to bombs that were going to go off if I didn't get to the next room in 30 seconds?

00:13:28.780 --> 00:13:32.980
I don't know. It's just, like, well, games have timers, so this game has to have a timer.

00:13:32.980 --> 00:13:34.460
And we haven't really shaken that yet.

00:13:34.620 --> 00:13:39.160
Right. And how did you feel just about, like, forget the timer thing, but just progression throughout this game?

00:13:39.320 --> 00:13:46.720
Did you feel like you were, like, climbing the ladder of skill and difficulty and, like, being introduced to new things?

00:13:46.720 --> 00:13:51.820
I did, but I also don't think I needed a built-in mechanism to feel that way.

00:13:52.120 --> 00:13:54.800
I mean, I'm going to learn the game as I play it.

00:13:54.860 --> 00:14:00.200
I'm going to realize that I'm getting better as the game goes on.

00:14:00.220 --> 00:14:02.100
I don't need the heart system.

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:05.080
Yeah, this is a game that does really well through repetition, too, though.

00:14:05.640 --> 00:14:13.540
I think it's just the right amount of simplicity meets, like, diversity in your movesets, which is why I wish you would have had it all to begin with.

00:14:13.560 --> 00:14:22.180
Yeah. That I didn't mind dying and starting over again because it was only ever two enemies at a time on your screen.

00:14:22.180 --> 00:14:27.760
It's not this, like, fodder of enemies that you just have to figure out, like, well, how am I going to get through all this?

00:14:27.820 --> 00:14:30.960
And you can't get to the next stage until you beat them all and stuff like that.

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:39.220
What's funny is that the two people on screen, two enemies on screen at one time was just a limitation of the NES.

00:14:39.460 --> 00:14:41.760
I read that, yeah. And it winds up working out in its benefit, I feel.

00:14:42.080 --> 00:14:48.380
I think that it would have at least, like, again, with diversity of experience,

00:14:49.240 --> 00:14:59.500
it would have been nice to be able to throw in a couple screens where you have to fight one enemy of one kind and another of the other kind.

00:15:01.300 --> 00:15:10.200
But, obviously, we don't, because this is kind of a new genre right now, we don't, I'm not really, I don't really care about that.

00:15:10.440 --> 00:15:16.980
Yeah. Because this is, at least, this is fresh enough that I don't care that I have to fight two duplicates of the same guy right now.

00:15:17.060 --> 00:15:22.840
Right. And to be clear, I think that the two enemies on screen is the sweet spot in this kind of thing

00:15:22.840 --> 00:15:27.800
because you can deal with one person on the left and one person on the right and you do have to watch your back,

00:15:27.960 --> 00:15:32.740
but it's not overwhelming to the point where it's like there's four guys on screen

00:15:32.740 --> 00:15:38.860
and I'm barely understanding my placement on the Z-axis, you know, it's, I think...

00:15:39.720 --> 00:15:42.840
They will, if you're not careful, they will wobble you.

00:15:43.000 --> 00:15:44.300
Yeah, and they do, they do.

00:15:44.580 --> 00:15:52.060
I think there was something really funny that happened to me one time with, one of them had a bat and the other one was just normal.

00:15:52.580 --> 00:15:57.240
And I was just fighting the guy with the bat because you want to disarm him and then you pick up the,

00:15:57.240 --> 00:15:58.220
yeah, you want to pick up the bat.

00:15:58.220 --> 00:16:03.620
And I would punch him, the bat would fall, and then the guy behind me would punch me,

00:16:03.680 --> 00:16:06.220
and then the guy with the bat would pick up the bat.

00:16:06.340 --> 00:16:07.740
And it just kept happening.

00:16:07.900 --> 00:16:11.460
It was like a Looney Tunes episode where then I proceed to, you know,

00:16:11.520 --> 00:16:14.900
because he's in the middle of picking up the bat so that I punch him in the face.

00:16:15.060 --> 00:16:18.660
And I just did that until he died and then I took care of the other guy.

00:16:18.860 --> 00:16:26.920
But that brings up another thing that we haven't seen before and that's interactive items and, you know, tools that you can use here.

00:16:26.920 --> 00:16:34.280
Another thing that, you know, this is just looking back in hindsight, but another thing that I didn't look at the manual at all,

00:16:34.360 --> 00:16:37.420
but when I saw those objects, I just assumed I can pick this up.

00:16:37.500 --> 00:16:39.820
And I just assumed using the punch button.

00:16:39.840 --> 00:16:45.320
It's like we take that for granted, but I think that's actually like just really good design decisions from this team.

00:16:45.580 --> 00:16:50.220
Oh, yeah. To just know like, no, you shouldn't hit select to pick up an object.

00:16:50.360 --> 00:16:52.880
Like it should just be the same button that your fists are, you know?

00:16:53.000 --> 00:16:54.660
Exactly. I really like that.

00:16:54.920 --> 00:16:59.200
And, you know, for what it's worth, there's a, you know, there's a good variety of things.

00:16:59.320 --> 00:17:06.740
I mean, I'm sure we could have X for a little more, but I think like having the baseball bat and the whip and some of the bigger objects,

00:17:06.760 --> 00:17:08.200
you pick up a crate at some points.

00:17:08.520 --> 00:17:11.340
I like that there's also some weight to those bigger objects too.

00:17:12.060 --> 00:17:18.660
But, you know, those things help along the way because they almost make you feel like you're taking control over the situation.

00:17:18.660 --> 00:17:21.600
Once you like disarm the person and now you have the bat.

00:17:21.820 --> 00:17:33.660
And that level of interactivity with the level itself is sort of what makes me like a lot of these action titles and even the games outside of this genre.

00:17:33.660 --> 00:17:51.460
Like I remember really enjoying, I don't remember which wrestling game it was, but whatever the wrestling game was where you can like leave the ring and then go into the back and like pick up a chair that was just sitting in the locker room or something.

00:17:51.460 --> 00:17:54.420
I mean, those were later like N64 games and stuff like that.

00:17:54.520 --> 00:17:55.780
But yeah, those are perfect.

00:17:56.000 --> 00:17:56.860
Great example.

00:17:57.120 --> 00:18:10.060
And just like, yeah, like interactive battles in more recent games like Sleeping Dogs where you can use the, use a urinal and put their face in a urinal.

00:18:10.060 --> 00:18:23.380
Like this is sort of that like step one and just the interactivity of the level design extends itself to like how the actual surface you can walk on interacts with you as well.

00:18:23.520 --> 00:18:37.160
Like in the first level, you get into that weird conveyor belt area and you can actually just kill your enemies, but not just by punching them, but by knocking them down on the conveyor belt.

00:18:37.280 --> 00:18:38.380
Like, and they just fall to their death.

00:18:38.380 --> 00:18:45.400
Like I think that that's very creative and it really, it really increases my enjoyment of these kinds of games.

00:18:45.540 --> 00:18:54.220
Yeah. I think, I think if Renegade for us was impressive because we had Kung Fu to compare it to, then this game is just, it's on like the same scale.

00:18:54.420 --> 00:19:02.640
Like now Kung Fu looks like something from like Atari 2600 and Renegade looks like an early NES game.

00:19:02.920 --> 00:19:04.560
It really does look like from the 2600.

00:19:04.560 --> 00:19:13.260
But, but yeah, there, there's a, there's a, there's a, there's an interactivity to the environment, but also it helps make it all feel more alive.

00:19:13.480 --> 00:19:14.860
Yeah. For an NES game.

00:19:15.100 --> 00:19:24.400
Very much. Which I think is pretty cool because right now we've seen a lot of like, you know, and this is a interesting example, but in Wizards and Warriors, right?

00:19:24.560 --> 00:19:31.280
You go to a lot of different places and you see yourself move around on the map, but I never really felt like I was at any of those places.

00:19:31.680 --> 00:19:34.060
You know, like I'm jumping around on tree stumps.

00:19:34.080 --> 00:19:35.120
I'm in a cave.

00:19:35.660 --> 00:19:36.840
I think those things are happening.

00:19:36.840 --> 00:19:43.680
But when you're like in Double Dragon and even if you're at like the castle at the end, which is like kind of weird.

00:19:43.940 --> 00:19:45.100
It's a hidden fortress.

00:19:45.560 --> 00:19:46.660
Okay. A hidden fortress.

00:19:46.940 --> 00:19:56.440
Or when you're in these caves, it's just having, having that stuff like be available to you makes it all feel more, more alive.

00:19:56.440 --> 00:20:01.360
Yeah. It has a sense of place and it has a sense of maybe not being lived in.

00:20:01.500 --> 00:20:12.100
Like this isn't, this isn't like an Elder Scrolls game, but it definitely has more external logic and not just video game logic of enemy placement.

00:20:12.340 --> 00:20:16.240
Yeah. It's as simple as like how in Super Mario Brothers, when you go down the pipe, it's like, cool.

00:20:16.320 --> 00:20:17.560
Now I understand why I'm in the underground.

00:20:17.740 --> 00:20:20.020
It's like sometimes you just need that connective tissue.

00:20:20.160 --> 00:20:21.500
That's what made Castlevania so great.

00:20:21.600 --> 00:20:25.440
Exactly. You understood where you were going, even if it was nonsensical.

00:20:25.440 --> 00:20:26.680
It was like, cool.

00:20:26.800 --> 00:20:31.860
I mean, like I feel like I just got out of that room and now I'm in the, you know, I'm getting closer to the tower.

00:20:32.120 --> 00:20:56.410
Yeah. Wow.

00:21:39.720 --> 00:21:41.860
We should probably talk about the boss battles

00:21:41.860 --> 00:21:45.320
because this was, for me, the moment where I remembered

00:21:45.320 --> 00:21:47.180
that I'm not good at beat-em-ups.

00:21:47.400 --> 00:21:49.580
So what are we considering a boss battle?

00:21:49.600 --> 00:21:53.880
I would start with even though he later just becomes a generic enemy,

00:21:55.200 --> 00:21:57.340
the Abadobo, I think his name is.

00:21:57.640 --> 00:21:59.760
So the big, like, the thing-looking guy.

00:21:59.880 --> 00:22:00.720
Yes. Okay.

00:22:00.720 --> 00:22:03.660
And he shows up in the end of Mission 1, I think.

00:22:03.820 --> 00:22:06.860
Yeah. Ababo is his name.

00:22:07.000 --> 00:22:08.120
Adobo. Abobo?

00:22:08.120 --> 00:22:10.320
Abobo. Okay.

00:22:10.700 --> 00:22:11.780
Abobo, Abobo.

00:22:11.880 --> 00:22:13.460
Yeah. Abobo-bop-bop-bop-bop-bop.

00:22:14.120 --> 00:22:15.120
That works, too.

00:22:15.280 --> 00:22:18.580
He definitely has the intimidation factor going for him.

00:22:18.860 --> 00:22:23.000
But I think, so this is the beginning of level one, you said?

00:22:23.000 --> 00:22:26.080
Yeah, that's what, you're still on the conveyor belt and everything at the end of this.

00:22:26.120 --> 00:22:28.680
See, that was easy for me because that was the first time that I'm like,

00:22:29.100 --> 00:22:34.740
wait, I know that I can just beat him by knocking him down on the conveyor belt

00:22:34.740 --> 00:22:36.060
and I won't really have to fight him.

00:22:36.260 --> 00:22:42.560
Right, right. So I think that later in the game when you have to fight more than one

00:22:42.560 --> 00:22:47.140
and he just becomes like a regular goon, then it's more, it's harder and it's more annoying.

00:22:47.580 --> 00:22:53.860
Yeah, I think that happens around level three, which is where you also start to see an excess

00:22:53.860 --> 00:22:57.660
of just like screens to get to things.

00:22:58.020 --> 00:23:01.920
So like at first it was just like, you know, all right, face two of these guys,

00:23:02.240 --> 00:23:06.220
move a little bit forward, face four of these guys, move a little bit forward, then two again.

00:23:06.600 --> 00:23:13.120
By level three, you're facing like five of one thing, three of another thing, four of one thing.

00:23:13.120 --> 00:23:18.300
You know, like it just keeps going and then you have to fight the two Bobos.

00:23:18.460 --> 00:23:24.820
And that's like, for me, that's kind of like, I'm not good enough at this genre to deal with this.

00:23:24.960 --> 00:23:27.520
I just, I just spammed jump kicks.

00:23:27.780 --> 00:23:29.500
Jump kicks are good. I'm a fan of them.

00:23:29.840 --> 00:23:35.540
They dodged most of them, but I was able to get out of the way of like a counter attack quickly.

00:23:35.680 --> 00:23:37.840
And that's worth mentioning too, the dodging thing.

00:23:37.840 --> 00:23:43.120
They're smarter AI for sure in the Bobos than in most of the other enemies.

00:23:43.460 --> 00:23:49.620
But, you know, I'm not saying that the difficulty of these guys like makes it a worse game.

00:23:49.740 --> 00:23:55.980
I think it's just, it was the revealing factor to me that I was kind of like cruising for a while

00:23:55.980 --> 00:24:01.380
and not actually like being good at this, at this genre or this game in specific.

00:24:01.380 --> 00:24:11.460
Yeah, I think the first, I don't remember if this is mission one or mission two, but after you climb up this building with a bunch of ladders.

00:24:11.680 --> 00:24:12.960
That's mission two, I believe.

00:24:13.020 --> 00:24:21.020
Okay. So you have like a mini boss, I guess, of the guy that, I don't know how to describe him.

00:24:21.100 --> 00:24:22.000
He's purple. Yeah.

00:24:22.180 --> 00:24:24.680
His name in the manual is Chintai.

00:24:25.040 --> 00:24:31.200
Okay. And yes, he's the purple one and that's after you fight the Lindas, which are the female characters in the game.

00:24:31.360 --> 00:24:35.000
I think that's really funny. It's like, what's a vicious name for a woman?

00:24:35.360 --> 00:24:44.280
Linda. And I remember that was the first fight that like it didn't feel like I was just counting how many times I knocked him down

00:24:44.280 --> 00:24:46.740
and then he, oh, he should start flashing and go away soon.

00:24:46.740 --> 00:24:59.400
It actually felt like a brawl because I didn't know his pattern and there was, it was mostly just raw reaction time and, and that kind of experience.

00:24:59.400 --> 00:25:01.000
I don't know how you felt about that.

00:25:01.120 --> 00:25:02.220
Would you count that as a boss?

00:25:02.360 --> 00:25:03.200
I would count that as a boss.

00:25:03.320 --> 00:25:05.080
It is, it is the end of that mission.

00:25:05.320 --> 00:25:09.500
I would count a boss anything where like all of a sudden you're introduced to the next mission.

00:25:09.700 --> 00:25:11.500
You know, you're facing him alone too.

00:25:11.500 --> 00:25:18.920
Uh, I think what was the only thing that was disappointing here was, uh, Chintai was another one that like I had trouble going up against.

00:25:19.020 --> 00:25:26.800
But when you look at like strategy guides on multiple different sites too, a lot of them have just figured out like very clever ways to like deal with this.

00:25:26.800 --> 00:25:27.620
Yeah, they can choose him.

00:25:27.840 --> 00:25:30.320
Yeah. And stuff like that for, for almost all the enemies though.

00:25:30.460 --> 00:25:34.980
And it seems like it's like, well, is that the best way to play this game?

00:25:35.640 --> 00:25:45.020
And, and if so, I guess what I mean is, is like everybody kind of agrees that it'd be better to cheese on these people than it would ever be to actually fight them.

00:25:45.540 --> 00:25:46.700
You know, like head on.

00:25:46.920 --> 00:25:53.200
Yeah. The one way that I've seen to beat the second boss, this Chintai, which isn't even cheesing.

00:25:53.320 --> 00:25:57.760
It's just like, it's just a glitch of, of going down the building again.

00:25:57.760 --> 00:25:58.960
And he just despawns.

00:25:59.080 --> 00:26:00.320
Oh, right. Okay. Yeah.

00:26:00.400 --> 00:26:01.540
I remember seeing that one as well.

00:26:01.620 --> 00:26:03.740
That's like, that's actually considered like a secret.

00:26:03.740 --> 00:26:08.640
Uh, and on some of the like guides, it's like, Oh, did you know you could do this?

00:26:08.820 --> 00:26:13.080
And you know, yeah, I think I'm fine with that stuff.

00:26:13.120 --> 00:26:18.740
And I'm fine with like, you know, figuring out the fastest ways to take enemies down.

00:26:18.940 --> 00:26:29.420
I, I meant that more as a comment of, is it like, does it say something about the game that no one's recommending like an actual way to just like fighting him as you would.

00:26:29.520 --> 00:26:31.060
Right. Be sure to die.

00:26:31.280 --> 00:26:32.200
Like you can't dodge.

00:26:32.420 --> 00:26:33.760
Yeah. You know, be sure to guard.

00:26:33.900 --> 00:26:40.240
You can't guard. I think that that's something that we haven't talked about yet that we're not thinking about it because we haven't played enough of these.

00:26:40.600 --> 00:26:45.100
But if we ever play a game with guard, going back to something like this is going to be tough.

00:26:45.400 --> 00:26:46.500
Yeah. That's true.

00:26:46.700 --> 00:26:50.400
The ability to just like cross your arms and have some invincibility is nice.

00:26:50.660 --> 00:26:52.620
And they punish you in games with guard too.

00:26:52.740 --> 00:26:56.980
Like usually if you guard too much, like a third punch will still knock you down.

00:26:57.220 --> 00:27:02.060
Yeah. So I, I think this game could have used something like that, but I'm not sure how they would have done it.

00:27:02.160 --> 00:27:09.420
I guess, I guess it doesn't really, I didn't really think of this because when I played these games, I was never very good and I never guarded.

00:27:09.820 --> 00:27:10.680
Right. I never knew.

00:27:10.680 --> 00:27:15.220
Even in, you know, say like Street Fighter 2 or something like that.

00:27:15.520 --> 00:27:17.100
I never thought to guard. It's really aggressive.

00:27:17.580 --> 00:27:20.560
Right. It's difficult to reprogram your mind like that.

00:27:20.720 --> 00:27:23.020
Yeah. But yeah.

00:27:23.300 --> 00:27:37.360
Yeah. And I think it's, it's worth mentioning that at this point, you know, if we're going all the way to the bosses, we might as well go to the end of the game where the final boss, Willie, is the guy who kidnapped your girlfriend and everything like that.

00:27:37.500 --> 00:27:45.640
And in the arcade version, it was kind of crazy because you would fight him, but also like enemies would just keep spawning.

00:27:45.680 --> 00:27:46.980
But you had to take care of Willie.

00:27:47.100 --> 00:27:48.920
Like everybody else would just run off if he died.

00:27:49.060 --> 00:27:50.600
Willie's the guy with the machine gun, right?

00:27:50.600 --> 00:27:53.400
Yes. Okay. The only one with a gun in the game.

00:27:54.720 --> 00:28:05.500
And in the, in the version here though, it's Willie just shows up at the end after you've taken out like two of Bobos, two Chintais, two Lindas, two of, like you have to take on.

00:28:05.500 --> 00:28:06.360
Williams. Yeah.

00:28:06.460 --> 00:28:12.120
You have to take on like two of all of these types and then finally you fight Willie and he's by himself.

00:28:12.180 --> 00:28:16.540
And I just think that's interesting because in the arcade version, it was more of just like a try to survive.

00:28:16.540 --> 00:28:20.840
Well, again, like they can't put more than one kind of enemy on the screen.

00:28:21.060 --> 00:28:22.740
So they had to redesign that.

00:28:22.860 --> 00:28:29.700
But let's, let's jump back a bit and talk about the actual design of some of these levels.

00:28:29.860 --> 00:28:33.920
Sure. Um, so level one, it's pretty standard.

00:28:34.160 --> 00:28:35.500
It's pretty intuitive.

00:28:35.700 --> 00:28:52.960
It has, you know, some, some nicks and crannies, but level three, I almost just stopped playing the game because of level three, because I didn't realize that after you fight the two of Bobos that jump out of the cave, jump out of the mountain and turn into a cave.

00:28:52.960 --> 00:28:59.580
Uh, that you shouldn't keep going right because the game just, the level just loops.

00:28:59.880 --> 00:29:01.880
Right. With the, with the platforms, right?

00:29:02.080 --> 00:29:04.040
After you get on those floating platforms.

00:29:04.480 --> 00:29:08.660
That, that, that is level, that is level three, but that's like the second part.

00:29:09.040 --> 00:29:10.640
So no, no, no, no. The first part.

00:29:10.920 --> 00:29:12.540
Out, when you're outside in the forest.

00:29:12.760 --> 00:29:20.560
Yep. They, you, you, you walk across the bridge and then you fight some dudes that, uh, I don't know.

00:29:20.620 --> 00:29:21.880
They just look like they're wearing jeans.

00:29:21.880 --> 00:29:23.680
Those are the, those are the Williams.

00:29:24.040 --> 00:29:25.580
Okay. But like, I know what you're talking about.

00:29:25.640 --> 00:29:27.640
The bridge was what, this is the, this is the bridge.

00:29:27.780 --> 00:29:30.340
It's not the bridge. But no, but this is the bridge scene I was talking about earlier.

00:29:30.800 --> 00:29:37.000
And basically that's the only, like, that for me was the recognition that something was, yeah.

00:29:37.000 --> 00:29:38.800
Something was up. Yeah.

00:29:38.980 --> 00:29:45.300
So I, I, there, I think there should be, cause like they give you the, the thumbs up to keep moving forward.

00:29:45.940 --> 00:29:51.220
But so maybe if you're, if it's going to be in a screen that has a cave, maybe like point to the cave.

00:29:51.220 --> 00:29:55.320
Yeah. They don't, they don't imply that because the Bobos broke through.

00:29:55.620 --> 00:30:00.060
Yeah. To make those holes that it was never implied that you should enter them.

00:30:00.380 --> 00:30:03.420
Yeah. Uh, so yeah, that is, that is very strange.

00:30:03.420 --> 00:30:10.480
Uh, and I think depending on like which one you enter, you also like enter a different part.

00:30:10.960 --> 00:30:12.860
Yeah. Of the, of the cave.

00:30:13.060 --> 00:30:18.100
I went right into the, I think both times I played through this, I went into the purple cave.

00:30:18.540 --> 00:30:21.140
Yeah. With the falling stalactites.

00:30:21.500 --> 00:30:22.620
Or is it stalagmites?

00:30:22.940 --> 00:30:24.040
Uh, no idea.

00:30:24.060 --> 00:30:26.860
But there, oh, it does say actually here somewhere.

00:30:27.140 --> 00:30:28.980
Hold on. It's stalactites.

00:30:29.480 --> 00:30:30.380
Stalactites. Yes.

00:30:30.800 --> 00:30:33.620
Yep. Uh, you know, which are mandatory in caves.

00:30:33.640 --> 00:30:37.340
It seems like at this point in NES games, it has to be something that falls that if you

00:30:37.340 --> 00:30:38.860
get close to it, it just starts falling.

00:30:39.220 --> 00:30:43.060
Exactly. Um, the platforms were weird though, because they looked like they were taken out of like

00:30:43.060 --> 00:30:44.600
Contra or something like that.

00:30:44.600 --> 00:30:48.420
It's also weird to just see, to see platforming in, in this game.

00:30:48.600 --> 00:30:49.760
It shouldn't be there in my opinion.

00:30:50.460 --> 00:30:55.740
Um, but yeah, then there's a, there's also like a whole nother part to this cave too with

00:30:55.740 --> 00:30:57.720
some waterfalls and stuff like that.

00:30:57.960 --> 00:30:59.400
Yeah. It's got, it's, it's okay.

00:30:59.660 --> 00:31:05.600
Yeah, it's okay. It's okay. But I do agree that it is a little strange that if you, if you don't go into those holes,

00:31:05.860 --> 00:31:08.380
you will just loop back and come across the bridge again.

00:31:08.380 --> 00:31:12.820
And I think that's why the bridge is probably there to remind you of like, hey, you noticed,

00:31:12.820 --> 00:31:14.280
uh, you'd been doing this right.

00:31:14.480 --> 00:31:15.540
Like you should just stop.

00:31:15.760 --> 00:31:16.580
It should just stop.

00:31:16.700 --> 00:31:22.820
It should stop. You know, if there's a thumb that tells you to keep going, that thumb should also like

00:31:22.820 --> 00:31:27.480
be able to like point into the cave or, you know, use an index finger with your thumb.

00:31:27.660 --> 00:31:30.960
Yeah. Well, it's usually it's thumb for the other thing.

00:31:31.060 --> 00:31:31.900
Like get out of here.

00:31:32.180 --> 00:31:33.380
Like you're good.

00:31:33.920 --> 00:31:36.660
Um, for those of you that don't know what we're talking about, it's literally just a giant

00:31:36.660 --> 00:31:38.860
thumb that like appears on the screen.

00:31:39.000 --> 00:31:42.800
Instead of like a, an arrow that says you can go to the next screen now, which is

00:31:42.820 --> 00:31:43.740
just a thumb. Yeah.

00:31:43.940 --> 00:31:45.460
It's like hitching a taxi ride.

00:31:45.660 --> 00:31:47.640
Yeah. Yep. Uh, okay.

00:31:47.840 --> 00:31:50.060
Any, any other thoughts on the level design?

00:31:53.100 --> 00:31:57.300
Um, so were we just going to talk about the final level as its own thing?

00:31:57.380 --> 00:31:59.080
Yes. Uh, that's what I was getting to.

00:31:59.240 --> 00:32:00.280
Okay. Let's talk about that.

00:32:00.400 --> 00:32:03.880
Okay, cool. So you, you're, you're calling it a hidden fortress.

00:32:04.320 --> 00:32:07.500
Um, I'm calling it this weird, like dungeon cave.

00:32:07.500 --> 00:32:11.140
There's like, it's just so unfitting for the rest of the game, but I guess going into a

00:32:11.140 --> 00:32:12.260
cave was weird enough.

00:32:12.440 --> 00:32:17.280
So like naturally you would lead up into like this place filled with skulls and, uh, Mike

00:32:17.280 --> 00:32:18.340
Wazowski eyes.

00:32:18.500 --> 00:32:20.560
This, I mean, this is like organized crime.

00:32:20.660 --> 00:32:22.080
Obviously you're going to find this stuff.

00:32:22.400 --> 00:32:25.240
Right. Oh, where else are you going to see this stuff?

00:32:25.620 --> 00:32:29.460
